<<<
12. Quantity words. There are heaps more to say, but I must not tax your space further.
===
**12.** Quantity words. *There are heaps more to say, but I must not tax your space further.*
>>>

<<<
The plurals heaps & lots used colloquially for a great amount now always take a singular verb unless a plural noun with of is added: There is heaps of ammunition, but There are heaps of cups; There is lots to do, but Lots of people think so.
===
The plurals *heaps & lots* used colloquially for a great amount now always take a singular verb unless a plural noun with *of* is added: *There* is *heaps of ammunition,* but *There* are *heaps of cups;* *There* is *lots to do,* but *Lots of people* think so.
>>>

<<<
Compare the use of half in Half of it is rotten, but Half of them are rotten.
===
Compare the use of *half* in *Half of it* is *rotten,* but *Half of them* are *rotten.*
>>>

